1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
The scheduler makes one decision, again and again: which ready process runs next.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
Five numbers describe a schedule: utilisation, throughput, turnaround, waiting and response.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
Turnaround is completion minus arrival. Waiting is turnaround minus the burst.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
Response time is the first slice only, which is why round robin can improve it without improving the average.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
FCFS runs processes in arrival order and never preempts: A, then B, then C, then D.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
The waiting times are 0, 8, 12 and 13, so the average waiting time is 8.25.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
This is the convoy effect: one long job at the head makes everyone behind it late.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
FCFS never starves anyone, and it cannot preempt, so a long job can block an interactive one.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
SJF picks the shortest burst among the processes that have arrived, and still never preempts.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
Waiting times 0, 11, 6 and 13: average 7.5, better than 8.25 for FCFS.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
SJF is optimal for average waiting time, and the proof is an exchange argument.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
The theorem assumes the bursts are known. In a real system they must be predicted.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
SRTF is SJF with preemption: at every arrival, compare remaining times.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
The chart is A 0 to 2, B 2 to 4, C 4 to 7, B 7 to 11, D 11 to 18, A 18 to 26.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
Average waiting falls to 6, but A waits 16 instead of 0.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
Its response time is the best of the four algorithms: 1.25 on average.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
Round robin gives each process a fixed slice, then sends it to the back of the queue.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
The chart is A, B, C, A, D, B, A, D, and the average waiting time is 10.75.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
What round robin buys is response time: 3.75 against 8.25 for FCFS.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
A quantum at least as long as the longest burst makes round robin identical to FCFS.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
Priority scheduling always runs the highest-priority ready process.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
Preemptive priority lets a new higher-priority arrival take the CPU immediately.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
Starvation is not slowness. It is a schedule in which a process never runs at all.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
Aging raises a waiting process priority until it must be chosen.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
A multilevel feedback queue uses several queues with different quanta.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
Use your whole quantum and you are demoted; give up the CPU early and you stay.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
On this process set the average waiting time is 11.5, worse than every single-queue algorithm.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
What it buys is that all four processes start at once: response time 0 for each.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
Every switch costs time in which no user instruction runs.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
The fraction of time lost is s over q plus s, so utilisation is q over q plus s.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
Halving the quantum doubles the number of switches and doubles the loss.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
So the quantum is a compromise between response time and wasted CPU.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
A process alternates CPU bursts and I/O bursts, so the CPU scheduler sees only part of its life.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
With one process the CPU idles during every I/O burst; with several it can be filled.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
Favouring short bursts keeps I/O-bound processes responsive and fills the CPU.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
The syllabus names CPU and I/O scheduling together; the disk side gets its own lesson.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Step one: fix the rules. Preemptive or not, and are the bursts known?

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
Step two: draw the chart from the arrival events, one segment per run.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Step three: read completion times off the chart, then subtract.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
Five traps: the divisor, idle time, ties, the metric, and the ignored switch cost.

